WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.569 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Let's say that you normally
go to bed at 10:00 PM,

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.204
and you wake up at 6:00 AM,

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:07.207
but tomorrow morning you've
got an early morning flight,

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.943 align:start size:97% position:3%
so you have to wake up at
4:00 AM rather than 6:00 AM.

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:11.945
How much sleep have you lost?

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.446
Well, you've lost two hours

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:17.450
of your eight hour night of
sleep, so you've lost 25%.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.718
Yes and no.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.387
You've lost 25% of
your total sleep,

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:24.591
but based on the structure of
sleep that we just described,

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:28.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
you may have lost 50, 60,
70% of all of your REM sleep,

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:30.196
all of your dream sleep.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:33.933 align:start size:97% position:3%
So there are real implications
to understanding that.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.070
(upbeat mellow music)

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.876 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.445 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
is a professor of
neuroscience and psychology

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.947 line:0%
at the University of
California, Berkeley,

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.950
Director of the Center
for Human Sleep Science,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
and host of "The
Matt Walker Podcast."

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.624 align:start size:97% position:3%
Dr. Matthew Walker, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:58.792
- Pleasure to be with you, Dave.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.060
Thanks for having me.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.528
- Well, I'm thrilled
that we're doing this

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
because as you know, we're
airing this in August,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.466 align:start size:97% position:3%
but right now it's the last
couple of days of July.

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:11.471
I am on my 30 day digital and
really societal sabbatical,

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:13.640
and I'm going to work
on a lot of things,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.975
diet, exercise, digital detox,

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.411
but also work on
some sleep stuff,

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.780
and that is your
area of expertise.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I guess before I
ask you anything else,

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:25.819
how does one become
curious and enamored

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:29.756 align:start size:97% position:3%
and eventually live their life
to tell people about sleep

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.224
of all things?

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:34.327 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Yeah, I think myself,
like many of my colleagues,

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:36.463 line:0%
we are all accidental
sleep researchers.

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.566 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
You know, I don't think
when we were kids,

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:41.367
and you were going
around the classroom,

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.635
and the teacher was
saying, you know,

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:44.771
"What would you like to
be when you grow up,"

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:46.573 align:start size:84% position:16%
no one was shooting
their hand and saying,

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:49.109
"I'd really love to be
a sleep researcher."

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:53.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I fell into it by accident
when I was doing my PhD.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.849 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was studying different
types of dementia

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.051 align:start size:84% position:16%
and trying to diagnose
them very early on,

00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:59.385
failing miserably,

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.322
and I realized I should
be measuring my patients

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:05.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
when they were asleep rather
than when they were awake

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:06.993
to measure their
brainwave activity.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.863 align:start size:88% position:13%
Started doing that,
got some great results,

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:12.398
and realized, that
was 20 years ago,

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:13.867
realized that at the time,

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.236
no one could answer a
very simple question.

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:17.737
Why do we sleep?

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:21.007
And the best answer we had
back then was that we sleep

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.210
to cure sleepiness, which is

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.745
the factual equivalent.
- I guess that works.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.113
- You know, that's like saying,

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:29.249
well, you eat to cure hunger.

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:33.052 align:start size:97% position:3%
No, you eat for a whole
plethora of different reasons.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:36.389
Now, 20 years later, due to the
work of all of my colleagues

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.358 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we've done a
little bit too I hope,

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.327
we've had to upend the question.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.462
We've had to ask,

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that any operation
of your brain,

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.967 align:start size:97% position:3%
and is there any physiological
system in your body

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:51.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
that isn't wonderfully
enhanced when you get sleep

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:54.274
or demonstrably impaired
when you don't get enough?

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:56.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so far the answer
seems to be no.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.744
- Yeah. All right.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.245
Well, there's a whole
bunch of questions

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.513
that I want to ask you
that are going to revolve

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:03.183
sort of around my sleep
habits and my nap habits

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:06.452 align:start size:84% position:16%
and this thing, what's
in this coffee cup,

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.321 align:start size:88% position:13%
but right now, which you
probably can guess...

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.190
- I'm so glad that you're
drinking decaffeinated.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.558
(both laughing)

00:03:11.558 --> 00:03:12.692
- I honestly don't

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:13.927
think I've ever.
- Au contraire.

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:15.461
- Yeah, I don't think
I've ever had decaf.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:18.298
But before we get into any
of any of those specifics,

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:19.666 align:start size:97% position:3%
can you just talk to me
sort of broadly about sleep?

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:22.001
You know, we hear about
sort of like, you know,

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:25.305
the type of sleep that you're
having throughout the night.

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:26.573
What's the most important?

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:28.775
When are you getting the
most restorative stuff?

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.276
All that.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:32.478
- Yeah, so sleep, at
least in human beings,

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.581
and in fact, in all
mammalian species,

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:37.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
is broadly separated
into two types.

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:38.985
We have non-rapid
eye movement sleep,

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:42.322 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and then we have rapid
eye movement sleep.

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:44.524 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And those two types
of sleep will play out

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:47.827 line:0%
in this beautiful sort of
battle for brain domination

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.662
throughout the night.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:53.233
And then the cerebral war
is going to be won and lost

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:56.669
between non-REM and
REM every 90 minutes.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.205
And then it's just going to
be replayed every 90 minutes

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:03.676
to create your standard sort
of Dave Rubin sleep cycle.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.645
What's different, however,

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:09.549
is the ratio of non-REM to REM
within those 90 minute cycles

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.451
as you move across the night.

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:13.386
So what I mean by that is

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:15.588
in the first half of last night,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
the majority of your
90 minute cycles

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:21.060
were comprised of lots
of deep non-REM sleep

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.795
and very little REM sleep,

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:25.732
but as you push through to
the second half of the night,

00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:28.801
that seesaw balance
actually shifts,

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.804
and now the majority of
those 90 minute cycles

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:34.540
is comprised much
more of REM sleep.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:35.775
And REM sleep is the stage

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.311 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we typically
associate with dreaming.

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:41.414
And I mention this
sort of architecture,

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:44.617 align:start position:0%
not just because it's hopefully
intellectually interesting,

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.719
but it has implications.

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:48.488
So let's say that you normally,

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.023
I'm just going to make this up,

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.792
and we'll hopefully hear what
your actual sleep schedule is,

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:56.329 align:start position:0%
but let's say that you
normally go to bed at 10:00 PM,

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:58.331
and you wake up at 6:00 AM,

00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:00.933
but tomorrow morning you've
got an early morning flight,

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:03.970 align:start size:97% position:3%
so you have to wake up at
4:00 AM rather than 6:00 AM.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.672
How much sleep have you lost?

00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:06.939
Well, you've lost two hours

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.975 align:start size:81% position:19%
of your eight hour
night of sleep,

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:11.177
so you've lost 25%.

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:12.445
Yes and no.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.981
You've lost 25% of
your total sleep,

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:18.318
but based on the structure of
sleep that we just described,

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:22.322 align:start size:97% position:3%
you may have lost 50, 60,
70% of all of your REM sleep,

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:23.856
all of your dream sleep.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:27.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
So there are real implications
to understanding that.

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:31.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
To your point though, which
one is more important,

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:34.167 align:start size:88% position:13%
all stages of sleep
are actually important.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.202
Different stages of sleep

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.838
do different things for
your brain and your body

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:43.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
at different times of night,
but we need all of them.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:45.445
You know, if, when
you think about sleep

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.914
as being such an
idiotic thing to do

00:05:47.914 --> 00:05:50.616
from an evolutionary
perspective, you know,

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.085
if Mother Nature
could have said,

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:55.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well, I'm going to take
these three stages of sleep,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:57.490
and I'm just going to remove
them so he saves some time,"

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.093
and there's no harm
to the organism,

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:04.130 align:start position:0%
then she would have done it
hundreds, millions of years ago,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.300 align:start size:88% position:13%
but all stages of sleep
have been preserved

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.569 align:start size:81% position:19%
throughout the
evolutionary path.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:11.604
That means all of
them are important,

00:06:11.604 --> 00:06:13.539
and that's exactly
what we're finding.

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:14.974
- So is eight hours,

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:16.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that the magic
number that everybody

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:19.512
sort of still seems to
think is like, you know,

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:20.980
for the average person,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
eight hours is the number
that you want to get?

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:25.952
- There is a range.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:27.754
So what we recommend right now

00:06:27.754 --> 00:06:30.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
is somewhere between seven
to nine hours of sleep

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.494 align:start size:88% position:13%
is what the average
adult seems to require.

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:35.795
Once you get below seven,

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:37.897
we can typically
start to measure

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.700
impairments in your
brain and your body,

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.302 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you know, so much
so based on the evidence

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:45.638
that the number of people who
seem to be able to survive

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:47.907
on less than six hours of sleep

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:50.042
without showing any impairment

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.644
rounded to a whole number

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:54.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
and expressed as a
percent of the population

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:55.281
is actually zero.

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:57.784
- Interesting.

00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:59.619
So I'll give you a little bit
of my personal part of this

00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:01.154
on the eight hours.

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:04.323
When I started writing
my first book last year,

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:06.459
really over two years
ago at this point,

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:07.960
when I first started writing it,

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.062
I would find that I was
thinking about it so much

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:11.497
that I would go to sleep,

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:13.499 align:start size:97% position:3%
let's say somewhere between
like 11 and 12:00 PM,

00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:16.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
or, you know,
midnight, basically,

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:17.570
I'd be thinking,

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:19.038 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then I'd wake up early
with the same thoughts

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:19.972
on my mind.

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:21.474
So I'd wake up at like 6:00 AM,

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:24.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then from 6:00 AM till
literally like 3:00 PM,

00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:26.345
I could just write and write
and write, write, write, write,

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:30.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then it would be like,
man, I need a break right now.

00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:33.519
And I started taking sort of
20 minute or half hour naps

00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:36.255
around 3:00 PM, and I
still try to do that.

00:07:36.255 --> 00:07:38.791 align:start position:0%
I can't always do it obviously,
but I try to do that.

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:42.161
And I'm curious, if you can
move some of it into a nap

00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:45.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
later in the day, do you
need it continuously?

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:47.166
- That's a beautiful question.

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:49.836
So firstly, there
is some importance

00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:52.405
about the continuity of sleep,

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:54.140
and what I mean by
that is at night,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
for that six hours
that you were getting,

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.211
if it's very choppy sleep,

00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:01.447 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's very sort of staccato,
and it's broken up,

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:04.550 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's peppered with
lots of awakenings,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.353
that's not good quality sleep.

00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:10.723
But let's go back to your
example or your lived life,

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:12.058
which is, you know,
six hours of sleep

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:13.993
and then a nap during the day.

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:15.094
Is that okay?

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:19.131
I think the first thing to
note is that we human beings

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:21.400
may not have been
designed in the way

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.069
that most of us sleep right now,

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:25.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is what we call
monophasic sleep.

00:08:25.438 --> 00:08:29.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
In other words, one long
single bout of sleep at night.

00:08:29.609 --> 00:08:33.145 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because if I place
electrodes on your head,

00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:36.382
sometime between the sort
of one to 4:00 PM mark,

00:08:36.382 --> 00:08:37.850
for most people,

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:41.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
there is a drop in your
brain's level of alertness,

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:44.524
and it's pre-programmed.

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:46.292
It has nothing to do with
a big lunch, by the way.

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:47.593
Some people think that.

00:08:47.593 --> 00:08:49.462
But you know, in the
afternoon in meetings

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:52.565
you can start to see those
ugly head bobs that sort of,

00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:54.333
you know, it's a dead giveaway.

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:55.568
People falling prey.

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:56.636
- That's the three
o'clock feeling,

00:08:56.636 --> 00:08:57.803 align:start size:81% position:19%
right?
- That's exactly.

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Didn't those
5-hour Energy people

00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:00.473
made an awful lot of
money on this feeling?

00:09:00.473 --> 00:09:01.541
- They certainly did indeed,

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:03.209
but it's genetically hardwired.

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:06.445 align:start position:0%
As I said, it's pre-programmed,
it happens to all of us.

00:09:06.445 --> 00:09:09.482
What that means is that we
may not have been designed

00:09:09.482 --> 00:09:12.118 align:start size:88% position:13%
as a species to sleep
in a monophasic pattern.

00:09:12.118 --> 00:09:14.253
We may have been designed
to sleep in what we call

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:17.557
a biphasic pattern, which is
a long bout of sleep at night,

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:18.991
just as you're getting,

00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:23.362 align:start position:0%
and then a short sort of siesta
like nap in the afternoon.

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.798 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I think that
that's perfectly fine.

00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:28.000
In fact, we may be
designed to do that,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.069 align:start size:84% position:16%
but naps are a
double-edged sword.

00:09:30.069 --> 00:09:34.473 align:start size:97% position:3%
There is a dark side to
naps, and it's the following.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:37.577
When you're awake
during the day,

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:40.246 align:start size:88% position:13%
you build up all of
this healthy sleepiness,

00:09:40.246 --> 00:09:41.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's a chemical
that builds up,

00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:43.916
and it's been building up
in my brain and your brain

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:45.651
since the moment we
woke up this morning,

00:09:45.651 --> 00:09:47.320
and it's called adenosine.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:50.089
The more of it that builds
up, the sleepier you feel.

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:52.191
And then when you go
to sleep at night,

00:09:52.191 --> 00:09:54.160
the brain gets the
chance to evacuate

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
all of that sleep pressure,
all of that adenosine.

00:09:57.229 --> 00:09:58.631
So that after about eight hours,

00:09:58.631 --> 00:10:01.133 align:start size:88% position:13%
you wake up feeling
refreshed and restored.

00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:03.035
The problem with naps then

00:10:03.035 --> 00:10:05.871
is that if you take
a nap that's too long

00:10:05.871 --> 00:10:09.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
or too late in the
afternoon or in the evening,

00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:12.511
it just takes the edge
off that sleepiness.

00:10:12.511 --> 00:10:16.349
And it's a little bit like
a valve on a steam cooker.

00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:19.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, it just releases
some of that steam,

00:10:19.251 --> 00:10:22.021
and so now it's harder
for you to fall asleep

00:10:22.021 --> 00:10:24.256
and then stay asleep at night.

00:10:24.256 --> 00:10:27.793 align:start position:0%
So it's a little bit like
snacking before your main meal.

00:10:27.793 --> 00:10:32.598 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I think for you, I
would say if you are not

00:10:32.598 --> 00:10:34.400
struggling with sleep at night

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can nap
regularly during the day,

00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:38.371
the naps of about 20 minutes,

00:10:38.371 --> 00:10:42.241
sort of some time around,
stop before about 4:00 PM,

00:10:42.241 --> 00:10:43.709
that's great.

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:46.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
But if you are struggling
with sleep at night,

00:10:46.112 --> 00:10:47.880
then avoid naps during the day.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.815
Build up all of that
healthy sleepiness.

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:51.350
- Have you done any research,

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.952
or do you know of anyone
that's done any research?

00:10:52.952 --> 00:10:54.620
Sometimes what I'll
find is if I try

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.123 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
to just close my eyes for
20 minutes at three o'clock,

00:10:57.123 --> 00:10:58.424 line:0%
I don't necessarily fall asleep,

00:10:58.424 --> 00:10:59.925 line:0%
and it's actually pretty rare

00:10:59.925 --> 00:11:01.527 line:0%
that I would get
into a deep sleep,

00:11:01.527 --> 00:11:03.396 line:0%
but you know, I could be
in that quasi-sleep stage,

00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:04.864
but sometimes it's more just,

00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:06.332
I've just closed my eyes

00:11:06.332 --> 00:11:09.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just try not to think
about anything major,

00:11:09.201 --> 00:11:11.237 align:start size:88% position:13%
politics, the
craziness of the world,

00:11:11.237 --> 00:11:13.606 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that in and of itself
is almost enough of a reset.

00:11:13.606 --> 00:11:15.441
Have they done any
research on that,

00:11:15.441 --> 00:11:17.610
that just the process
of just 20 minutes

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:18.844
with your eyes closed

00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:20.946
and maybe just getting
into that initial phase

00:11:20.946 --> 00:11:22.348
could be enough?

00:11:22.348 --> 00:11:24.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, we have done
studies on that,

00:11:24.016 --> 00:11:25.918
and that's exactly
what we're finding,

00:11:25.918 --> 00:11:30.289 align:start position:0%
that even that quiet rest
where you have your eyes closed

00:11:30.289 --> 00:11:32.124
and you're somewhat disengaged.

00:11:32.124 --> 00:11:33.993
Now, if you look at
the brain itself,

00:11:33.993 --> 00:11:37.029
it's actually dipping like
a little beak into water.

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:39.899
It's dipping itself and
having a little soupcon

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:42.101
of sort of sleep
during that time.

00:11:42.101 --> 00:11:44.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
Most of that time, you're
not aware that you're asleep

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:46.372 align:start size:88% position:13%
because it's still
lighter stages of sleep,

00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:48.140
but it can be restorative.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:51.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
It can have benefits for
your mood, for your emotions,

00:11:51.243 --> 00:11:54.580
even some benefits for
cardiovascular health.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:58.718
Is it as potent as, you
know, getting into sleep?

00:11:58.718 --> 00:12:00.252
No, it's not.

00:12:00.252 --> 00:12:02.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
But is it better
than just constantly

00:12:02.788 --> 00:12:06.192 align:start size:97% position:3%
trying to power through and
grabbing another cup of coffee

00:12:06.192 --> 00:12:07.893
and you know, hope for the best?

00:12:07.893 --> 00:12:10.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
Absolutely, it's much
better than that.

00:12:10.096 --> 00:12:11.964
So it's all a range.

00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:14.734
I think there's a gradient
of different conscious states

00:12:14.734 --> 00:12:16.368
that our brain goes through,

00:12:16.368 --> 00:12:18.204
and some of them can
really be beneficial.

00:12:18.204 --> 00:12:20.272
It doesn't have to
all be about sleep.

00:12:20.272 --> 00:12:21.941
- All right, so you
mentioned coffee.

00:12:21.941 --> 00:12:23.509
I already showed
you the coffee cup.

00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:25.010
I'm a coffee guy.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:26.545
When I wake up in the morning,

00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:29.048
the first thing that I think
about is that cup of coffee.

00:12:29.048 --> 00:12:30.349
Sometimes I'm thinking about it

00:12:30.349 --> 00:12:32.017
as I'm going to bed
the night before,

00:12:32.017 --> 00:12:35.588
and I probably have a
second cup most days.

00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:37.556
Am I doing something wrong here?

00:12:37.556 --> 00:12:39.658 align:start size:84% position:16%
Am I overly
caffeinating my brain?

00:12:39.658 --> 00:12:41.093
Am I harming my sleep?

00:12:41.093 --> 00:12:44.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
Am I doing something too
unnatural to my normal rhythm?

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:46.132
- Based on what you just said,

00:12:46.132 --> 00:12:47.733
you're not doing anything wrong.

00:12:47.733 --> 00:12:48.701 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I would actually-
- All right.

00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:50.469
- recommend drink coffee,

00:12:50.469 --> 00:12:52.671
which sounds strange
from someone like me,

00:12:52.671 --> 00:12:55.908 align:start size:97% position:3%
but when it comes to coffee,
just like with drugs,

00:12:55.908 --> 00:12:59.578 align:start size:88% position:13%
the dose and the timing
makes the poison.

00:12:59.578 --> 00:13:02.148 align:start size:97% position:3%
So first, there are some
wonderful health associations

00:13:02.148 --> 00:13:07.153
with coffee, and that is, I
think, very strong evidence.

00:13:08.587 --> 00:13:10.790
By the way, it has nothing to
do with the caffeine though,

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:13.292
that the principle reason
you get health associations

00:13:13.292 --> 00:13:14.927
with the cup of coffee

00:13:14.927 --> 00:13:18.197
is because the coffee
bean contains most
of the antioxidants

00:13:18.197 --> 00:13:21.000
that people eat or consume

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.637
because our diets are not
particularly enriched,

00:13:24.637 --> 00:13:28.974
the way that we eat in this
modern era, in antioxidants.

00:13:28.974 --> 00:13:30.643
And it's left to
the coffee bean,

00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:32.378
the humble coffee bean,

00:13:32.378 --> 00:13:37.016
to carry the weight of most
people's antioxidant needs.

00:13:37.016 --> 00:13:39.919
So a cup of coffee
or two cups of coffee

00:13:39.919 --> 00:13:42.521
or even three cups of
coffee in the morning,

00:13:42.521 --> 00:13:44.056
that's just fine.

00:13:44.056 --> 00:13:47.326
Try to cut yourself off after
about midday for most people,

00:13:47.326 --> 00:13:49.128
and here's the reason why.

00:13:49.128 --> 00:13:53.098 align:start size:88% position:13%
Firstly, coffee or
caffeine has a half-life

00:13:53.098 --> 00:13:56.101
of about five to six hours.

00:13:56.101 --> 00:13:58.237 align:start size:88% position:13%
In other words, after
about five to six hours,

00:13:58.237 --> 00:14:01.106
50% of that caffeine is
still in your system,

00:14:01.106 --> 00:14:03.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
which means that caffeine
has a quarter-life

00:14:03.676 --> 00:14:05.477
of 10 to 12 hours.

00:14:05.477 --> 00:14:08.414
So if you have a cup
of coffee at midday,

00:14:08.414 --> 00:14:10.382
it would be the
equivalent of you

00:14:10.382 --> 00:14:11.750
getting into bed at midnight,

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.586 align:start size:84% position:16%
and before you
turn the light out,

00:14:13.586 --> 00:14:15.554
you swig a quarter
of a cup of coffee

00:14:15.554 --> 00:14:17.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you hope for
good night of sleep,

00:14:17.189 --> 00:14:20.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's probably
not going to happen.

00:14:20.125 --> 00:14:22.695 align:start position:0%
But even if you're one of those
people who says, you know,

00:14:22.695 --> 00:14:25.164
like, I can have an
espresso with dinner,

00:14:25.164 --> 00:14:29.001
I fall asleep fine, and I stay
asleep, so no harm, no foul.

00:14:29.001 --> 00:14:30.236
That's not quite true either

00:14:30.236 --> 00:14:33.138 align:start size:84% position:16%
because a cup of
coffee in the evening

00:14:33.138 --> 00:14:36.141
will decrease the amount
of deep sleep that you get,

00:14:36.141 --> 00:14:41.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
deep non-REM sleep, by
somewhere between 20 to 30%.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:44.917
Now, for me to take away that
amount of deep sleep from you,

00:14:44.917 --> 00:14:47.686
I would probably have to
age you by about 15 years,

00:14:47.686 --> 00:14:51.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
or you could just do it
every night with an espresso.

00:14:51.457 --> 00:14:54.827
So I think, you know, I
want to be clear here.

00:14:54.827 --> 00:14:57.229
I'm just a scientist,

00:14:57.229 --> 00:15:00.432 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm not here to tell
anyone how to live their life.

00:15:00.432 --> 00:15:01.967
That's not my job.

00:15:01.967 --> 00:15:05.170
All I want to do is, you
know, give you the science

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:08.007 align:start size:84% position:16%
behind sleep and
things like caffeine

00:15:08.007 --> 00:15:10.609 align:start size:88% position:13%
so that people can make
an informed choice.

00:15:10.609 --> 00:15:13.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, life is to be
lived to a certain degree.

00:15:13.112 --> 00:15:15.314
- Yeah. So when you meet
the people who are like,

00:15:15.314 --> 00:15:18.150
"Oh, you know, if I have a
cup of coffee after 4:00 PM,

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:19.652
I'm up all night"

00:15:19.652 --> 00:15:22.288
versus the guy that can have
the double espresso at 11,

00:15:22.288 --> 00:15:24.023
is that partly just
how they're wired?

00:15:24.023 --> 00:15:26.058 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, is that just
their physiology?

00:15:26.058 --> 00:15:27.459
- It is their physiology,

00:15:27.459 --> 00:15:29.194
and in fact, it's the genes.

00:15:29.194 --> 00:15:30.729
And we know these genes.

00:15:30.729 --> 00:15:32.564
There are several
different genes,

00:15:32.564 --> 00:15:35.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
and if you want to sort
of get a little bit nerdy,

00:15:35.701 --> 00:15:39.038
it's called cytochrome P450,

00:15:39.038 --> 00:15:42.841
and it determines the
speed of breakdown

00:15:42.841 --> 00:15:46.645
of decomposition essentially
of the caffeine in your system.

00:15:46.645 --> 00:15:49.882
Now, some people have a
version of that enzyme

00:15:49.882 --> 00:15:52.651
where they will break down
the caffeine very quickly.

00:15:52.651 --> 00:15:55.587 align:start position:0%
Other people, and
unfortunately, I'm one of them,

00:15:55.587 --> 00:15:58.123
have a version of that enzyme

00:15:58.123 --> 00:16:00.659 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the caffeine metabolism
is very, very slow.

00:16:00.659 --> 00:16:03.662
So if I have a cup of
coffee at, you know,

00:16:03.662 --> 00:16:07.566
9:00 AM in the morning
after waking up at 6:30

00:16:07.566 --> 00:16:08.834
or seven in the morning,

00:16:08.834 --> 00:16:10.869
even that usually leads to me

00:16:10.869 --> 00:16:12.771 align:start size:81% position:19%
not having a great
night of sleep.

00:16:12.771 --> 00:16:14.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
So there are
individual differences,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.809
and we understand the
genetic basis of those.

00:16:16.809 --> 00:16:19.478
- What about some of the
habits of good sleepers?

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:21.080
One of the things that
I've been trying to do

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:22.314
for the last couple of years,

00:16:22.314 --> 00:16:23.549
probably about two years now,

00:16:23.549 --> 00:16:26.185 align:start size:88% position:13%
is I try not to bring
my phone in the bedroom.

00:16:26.185 --> 00:16:28.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I don't want the phone
to be the last thing

00:16:28.187 --> 00:16:30.255
that I look at when my
head hits the pillow,

00:16:30.255 --> 00:16:32.124
and I don't want it to be
the first thing that I see

00:16:32.124 --> 00:16:33.726
when I wake up in the morning.

00:16:33.726 --> 00:16:35.194 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's been pretty
helpful for me.

00:16:35.194 --> 00:16:37.196 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do you guys recommend
certain habits,

00:16:37.196 --> 00:16:38.931
or have you found
that certain people

00:16:38.931 --> 00:16:40.599
just have better habits

00:16:40.599 --> 00:16:43.569 align:start size:97% position:3%
related to actually getting
into that deep sleep?

00:16:43.569 --> 00:16:45.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. I think you
mentioned a great one here,

00:16:45.938 --> 00:16:47.439
which is the phone.

00:16:47.439 --> 00:16:49.908
You know, the invasion of
technology into the bedroom

00:16:49.908 --> 00:16:54.913
has been a harmful influence
on our sleep in the modern era.

00:16:55.781 --> 00:16:56.782
And in a way though, you know,

00:16:56.782 --> 00:16:58.650
that genie is out the bottle,

00:16:58.650 --> 00:16:59.952
and it's not going
in any time soon.

00:16:59.952 --> 00:17:02.688
So there's no point in
me necessarily saying,

00:17:02.688 --> 00:17:05.958
you know, it's got to be a
technology free environment.

00:17:05.958 --> 00:17:07.226
What I would say though,

00:17:07.226 --> 00:17:09.762
is you've probably had
that experience where

00:17:09.762 --> 00:17:12.865
you know that you need to get
up for an early morning flight

00:17:12.865 --> 00:17:14.600
or there's something
that you have to do

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:16.402
ultra important
the next morning.

00:17:16.402 --> 00:17:18.470 align:start size:81% position:19%
You know that that
night of sleep

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:20.873
is just not going to
be the best night.

00:17:20.873 --> 00:17:22.107
And it's almost as though,

00:17:22.107 --> 00:17:23.609
if you've got to wake up
at five in the morning

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:27.813
for your flight, you wake up
at 4:58 before your alarm.

00:17:27.813 --> 00:17:29.048
- [Dave] Yep.

00:17:29.048 --> 00:17:30.582
- And the reason is
because the brain,

00:17:30.582 --> 00:17:33.085
it stays in this very
shallow form of sleep.

00:17:33.085 --> 00:17:35.054
It's not very restorative sleep.

00:17:35.054 --> 00:17:39.358
And it's a phenomenon that's
called anticipatory anxiety.

00:17:39.358 --> 00:17:41.627
In other words, you know
something is upcoming,

00:17:41.627 --> 00:17:45.230 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the brain goes into almost
this threat detection state

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:46.732
because of the anxiety,

00:17:46.732 --> 00:17:49.768
and your sleep is shallow
for understandable reasons.

00:17:49.768 --> 00:17:52.704
I mention this because
having your phone there

00:17:52.704 --> 00:17:54.973
is a kind of diet
version of that.

00:17:54.973 --> 00:17:57.476
You know, it's sort
of anxiety lite,

00:17:57.476 --> 00:18:00.079
L, I, T, E, for the next day,

00:18:00.079 --> 00:18:01.580
because you're right.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:04.850
For most of us, and I've been
guilty of this in the past,

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:06.418
you know, the first
thing that we do

00:18:06.418 --> 00:18:08.687
when we wake up in the
morning is reach across,

00:18:08.687 --> 00:18:11.657
and we swipe right, and
we open up the phone,

00:18:11.657 --> 00:18:13.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we know that there
is waiting for us,

00:18:13.692 --> 00:18:17.963
just a tsunami of anxiety,
of emails, social media,

00:18:17.963 --> 00:18:19.731 align:start size:84% position:16%
it all comes through.
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:18:19.731 --> 00:18:22.734
- And what that does is
that it trains your brain

00:18:22.734 --> 00:18:27.739 align:start size:97% position:3%
to expect that type of anxiety
jag each and every morning.

00:18:28.907 --> 00:18:30.375
And so when you go
to bed at night,

00:18:30.375 --> 00:18:33.278
you're not going to have that
restorative restful sleep.

00:18:33.278 --> 00:18:35.314 align:start position:0%
The other thing with technology
by the way in the evening

00:18:35.314 --> 00:18:38.383 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that it causes what's
called sleep procrastination.

00:18:38.383 --> 00:18:41.753
It's a real thing where, you
know, you're perfectly tired,

00:18:41.753 --> 00:18:43.021
you could fall asleep,

00:18:43.021 --> 00:18:44.289
but have your phone
there and you think,

00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:46.024
oh, I should just check
Facebook one less time,

00:18:46.024 --> 00:18:47.793 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm just going to
order that thing on Amazon,

00:18:47.793 --> 00:18:50.696 align:start size:97% position:3%
and, you know, oh, Dave
Rubin had this great section.

00:18:50.696 --> 00:18:52.498
I'm going to watch that.

00:18:52.498 --> 00:18:55.100
And all of a sudden,
it's 20 minutes later,

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you do that each
and every night,

00:18:56.702 --> 00:18:58.737
it's like compounding
interest on a loan,

00:18:58.737 --> 00:19:00.539
and it can really
degrade your sleep.

00:19:00.539 --> 00:19:02.508 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I would say
the rule of thumb,

00:19:02.508 --> 00:19:03.976 align:start size:81% position:19%
if you want to
try and hack that,

00:19:03.976 --> 00:19:06.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
is you could only use
your phone in the bedroom

00:19:06.678 --> 00:19:08.313
when you're standing up.

00:19:08.313 --> 00:19:11.150
And after about five
minutes of doing that,

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.852
as soon as you sit down
or you want to lie down,

00:19:13.852 --> 00:19:16.388
that's the time you got
to put the phone away.

00:19:16.388 --> 00:19:18.790
- Yeah, so you mentioned
the compounding interest

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:21.326 align:start size:97% position:3%
part of this, so when
people are in those phases,

00:19:21.326 --> 00:19:23.896 align:start size:97% position:3%
when you're just not getting
that good enough sleep,

00:19:23.896 --> 00:19:27.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're too busy,
life's crazy, anxiety,

00:19:27.332 --> 00:19:28.467
whatever it might be,

00:19:28.467 --> 00:19:31.837 align:start size:88% position:13%
what is the compounding
effects of that?

00:19:31.837 --> 00:19:33.005
What are those effects?

00:19:33.872 --> 00:19:35.274
- They are non trivial.

00:19:35.274 --> 00:19:38.377
I think, you know, we
used to think of sleep

00:19:38.377 --> 00:19:40.379 align:start size:84% position:16%
as the third pillar
of good health,

00:19:40.379 --> 00:19:42.614
alongside diet and exercise.

00:19:42.614 --> 00:19:45.450
Now, in fact, I was wrong
when I used to say that.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.085
It is actually the foundation

00:19:47.085 --> 00:19:49.354
on which those two
of the things sit,

00:19:49.354 --> 00:19:51.223
that's how essential it is.

00:19:51.223 --> 00:19:53.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's the reason that
we've now been finding

00:19:53.592 --> 00:19:56.328
significant links and
some of them causal

00:19:56.328 --> 00:19:58.197
between short sleep duration

00:19:58.197 --> 00:20:00.799
and things such as
Alzheimer's disease,

00:20:00.799 --> 00:20:03.502 align:start size:88% position:13%
depression,
cardiovascular disease,

00:20:03.502 --> 00:20:06.672
obesity, stroke, diabetes,

00:20:06.672 --> 00:20:09.408
numerous psychiatric
conditions as well.

00:20:09.408 --> 00:20:13.478
And so I think what this
tells us is that sleep is,

00:20:13.478 --> 00:20:16.682 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's not really an
optional lifestyle luxury.

00:20:16.682 --> 00:20:21.053
It is a non-negotiable
biological necessity.

00:20:21.053 --> 00:20:22.788
And if you use that sweet spot

00:20:22.788 --> 00:20:25.123
of seven to nine hours a night,

00:20:25.123 --> 00:20:26.792 align:start size:88% position:13%
there is an unfortunate
simple truth

00:20:26.792 --> 00:20:29.861 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the shorter your sleep,
the shorter your life.

00:20:29.861 --> 00:20:33.198
Short sleep predicts
all cause mortality.

00:20:33.198 --> 00:20:34.800
It is the elixir of life.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:39.538
You know, it's the Swiss Army
Knife of health as it were.

00:20:39.538 --> 00:20:40.839
- So as a scientist,

00:20:40.839 --> 00:20:42.874
I am sure you're
around scientists

00:20:42.874 --> 00:20:45.844
who would love to figure
out ways to make us operate

00:20:45.844 --> 00:20:48.413
at higher levels
with less sleep,

00:20:48.413 --> 00:20:50.649
to be, you know,
"more productive,"

00:20:50.649 --> 00:20:51.783
whether that is true or not,

00:20:51.783 --> 00:20:53.685
or I suppose it would depend on

00:20:53.685 --> 00:20:55.087
your definition of productive,

00:20:55.087 --> 00:20:56.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
but is there research
in that regard?

00:20:56.722 --> 00:20:58.890
Like, you know, if I could
get five more hours in a day,

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:00.592
just think about all the
things that I could do

00:21:00.592 --> 00:21:01.827
in those five hours?

00:21:01.827 --> 00:21:05.163
- Yeah, and the way
that sleep science

00:21:05.163 --> 00:21:09.801
has taught a lesson
around this question

00:21:09.801 --> 00:21:12.271
is a slightly different view.

00:21:12.271 --> 00:21:14.206 line:0%
The longer that you're awake...

00:21:14.206 --> 00:21:16.241 line:0%
So firstly, what we know
is that after a human being

00:21:16.241 --> 00:21:20.112 line:0%
has being awake for about
20 or 21 hours straight,

00:21:20.112 --> 00:21:22.981
you are as cognitively
impaired as you would be

00:21:22.981 --> 00:21:24.783
if you were legally drunk,

00:21:24.783 --> 00:21:28.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
which surprises me
that in the workplace,

00:21:28.854 --> 00:21:31.657 align:start size:88% position:13%
not all workplaces,
but commonly, you know,

00:21:31.657 --> 00:21:35.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's almost this sort of sleep
machismo attitude sometimes

00:21:35.594 --> 00:21:38.063 align:start size:97% position:3%
where we're quite braggadocio
of how little sleep

00:21:38.063 --> 00:21:39.298
that we're getting in,

00:21:39.298 --> 00:21:41.566
and employers will
sometimes co-opt

00:21:41.566 --> 00:21:45.604
and encourage that kind of
behavior from the employees.

00:21:45.604 --> 00:21:48.573
And what's funny to me is
that no employer would say,

00:21:48.573 --> 00:21:51.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Gosh, I've got this great
team I'm working with.

00:21:51.009 --> 00:21:52.377
They're drunk all the time,"

00:21:52.377 --> 00:21:53.512 align:start size:75% position:25%
you know.
(Dave laughing)

00:21:53.512 --> 00:21:55.380 align:start size:88% position:13%
But that's the
situation when your team

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:59.184 align:start size:97% position:3%
is trying to survive on
insufficient sleep in a way.

00:21:59.184 --> 00:22:02.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
So rather than answering your
question in the sense of,

00:22:02.688 --> 00:22:04.089
can we...

00:22:04.089 --> 00:22:07.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
Although I do have
several ideas and desires

00:22:07.225 --> 00:22:09.127
to try to do what you're saying,

00:22:09.127 --> 00:22:11.430
which is, can I compress sleep,

00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:13.131
can I kind of, you know,

00:22:13.131 --> 00:22:17.269
the analogy would be, can I
create a zip file for sleep?

00:22:17.269 --> 00:22:19.404
Can I shrink down the time,

00:22:19.404 --> 00:22:22.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we get everything
that we need in less time?

00:22:22.441 --> 00:22:25.344 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that that's a fool
hearty way of doing it though,

00:22:25.344 --> 00:22:27.612
because when you are well-slept,

00:22:27.612 --> 00:22:29.981
you are just much
more effective,

00:22:29.981 --> 00:22:32.417
and you are much more efficient.

00:22:32.417 --> 00:22:34.386
So to answer your question,

00:22:34.386 --> 00:22:38.390 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you want to get back
those five hours of your day,

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:40.425
don't think of sleep as a cost.

00:22:40.425 --> 00:22:43.328
Think of sleep as an
investment in tomorrow

00:22:43.328 --> 00:22:44.596
because when you are well slept,

00:22:44.596 --> 00:22:46.698
it's a little bit like cooking.

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:51.069
Why would you try to boil a
pot of water on medium heat

00:22:51.069 --> 00:22:53.872
when you could do it in
half the time on high?

00:22:53.872 --> 00:22:57.576
That is the consequence
of a good night of sleep.

00:22:57.576 --> 00:22:59.344
And so sometimes people say,

00:22:59.344 --> 00:23:01.079
"Look, the reason I can't get

00:23:01.079 --> 00:23:04.783 align:start position:0%
my seven to nine hours of sleep
is that I'm just so busy."

00:23:04.783 --> 00:23:06.385
And my response is sometimes,

00:23:06.385 --> 00:23:09.087 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Well, it may be that
you've got so much to do

00:23:09.087 --> 00:23:12.657 align:start size:97% position:3%
or so much left to do because
you're not well-slept.

00:23:12.657 --> 00:23:13.992
And if you were well-slept,

00:23:13.992 --> 00:23:15.594
your efficiency would increase,

00:23:15.594 --> 00:23:18.330
and you wouldn't have that
much left on your plate.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:21.133
That's what the science
is actually teaching us."

00:23:21.133 --> 00:23:24.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Where do things like the
pillow and the mattress

00:23:24.503 --> 00:23:27.072
and the temperature and
all that kind of stuff

00:23:27.072 --> 00:23:28.340
fit into this?

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:30.876
Because I know just from
YouTube and online life,

00:23:30.876 --> 00:23:34.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
almost every ad these
days is about a new pillow

00:23:34.012 --> 00:23:35.747 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's going to fix
all your sleep problems

00:23:35.747 --> 00:23:38.216
or a new mattress or
some other, you know,

00:23:38.216 --> 00:23:42.053
this new sheet has a
special thing in it

00:23:42.053 --> 00:23:43.555
that will keep you
cool if you're hot

00:23:43.555 --> 00:23:45.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
or keep you hot if you're cool
or just some other of that.

00:23:45.524 --> 00:23:48.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
There seems to be like a
real obsession with this now.

00:23:48.527 --> 00:23:51.129 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, and I don't mind
the obsession so much

00:23:51.129 --> 00:23:53.598
because in some ways it
draws attention to this.

00:23:53.598 --> 00:23:56.201
You know, sleep has been
the neglected stepsister

00:23:56.201 --> 00:23:57.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the health
conversation of today

00:23:57.903 --> 00:24:00.472
for the past 10, 20 years.

00:24:00.472 --> 00:24:01.940
So that's okay,

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:04.943 align:start position:0%
but, you know, there's a little
bit of snake oil out there,

00:24:04.943 --> 00:24:06.411
for sure.

00:24:06.411 --> 00:24:09.681
We don't have much data on
the effectiveness of pillows.

00:24:09.681 --> 00:24:12.017
Mattresses, there is some data,

00:24:12.017 --> 00:24:15.954 align:start position:0%
and mattresses are tough
because it's very individualized

00:24:15.954 --> 00:24:19.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on your weight,
your anatomical structure,

00:24:19.858 --> 00:24:23.061
are you in pain in terms
of your back, are you not,

00:24:23.061 --> 00:24:25.230
are you in pain for your neck?

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:29.935
And I do like, I mean, the
mattress is an interesting

00:24:29.935 --> 00:24:31.837
vehicle for sleep researchers

00:24:31.837 --> 00:24:35.140
because when you think about
the car that you drive today

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:37.275 align:start size:88% position:13%
versus the car that
you were driving maybe,

00:24:37.275 --> 00:24:39.411 align:start size:84% position:16%
or could have
bought 20 years ago,

00:24:39.411 --> 00:24:41.947
from a technology standpoint,
they are radically different.

00:24:41.947 --> 00:24:44.349
Nowadays your car is
packed full of sensors

00:24:44.349 --> 00:24:46.518
that measure just
about everything.

00:24:46.518 --> 00:24:48.320
Think about your
mattress, though.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:50.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, your mattress
today is much more similar

00:24:50.989 --> 00:24:53.091
to your mattress
of 20 years ago.

00:24:53.091 --> 00:24:55.060
Why is that the case?

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:58.163
Because we spend, you know,
hopefully seven to nine hours

00:24:58.163 --> 00:24:59.698
of sleep on our mattress.

00:24:59.698 --> 00:25:01.666
I very much hope that
most people don't spend

00:25:01.666 --> 00:25:03.168
that much time...

00:25:03.168 --> 00:25:05.837
Maybe they spend two hours
a day in the car at maximum.

00:25:05.837 --> 00:25:10.842
So yet we're happy to invest
tens of thousands of dollars

00:25:12.043 --> 00:25:14.045
in our car, which we
spend less time in,

00:25:14.045 --> 00:25:16.481 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we haven't got there
yet with mattresses.

00:25:16.481 --> 00:25:19.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think mattresses have
an untapped potential,

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:21.353 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there are a number
of companies now
that are doing this.

00:25:21.353 --> 00:25:24.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
The final thing you
mentioned is temperature.

00:25:24.055 --> 00:25:26.591
This is definitely one of
the things that you can use

00:25:26.591 --> 00:25:28.760
to help manipulate your sleep.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:31.296 align:start size:84% position:16%
It turns out that your
brain and your body

00:25:31.296 --> 00:25:33.164
need to drop the
core temperature

00:25:33.164 --> 00:25:35.333
by about two to three
degrees Fahrenheit,

00:25:35.333 --> 00:25:37.168
or about one degree Celsius,

00:25:37.168 --> 00:25:38.770
in order for you to fall asleep

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:41.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then stay asleep
soundly across the night.

00:25:41.606 --> 00:25:43.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's the reason you
will always find it easier

00:25:43.708 --> 00:25:46.611 align:start size:97% position:3%
to fall asleep in a room
that's too cold than too hot.

00:25:46.611 --> 00:25:48.980 align:start size:81% position:19%
So what we've been
finding is that

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:50.448
if you can use the mattress

00:25:50.448 --> 00:25:53.118
or just make sure
that your bedroom,

00:25:53.118 --> 00:25:56.388
the ambient temperature
is set appropriately.

00:25:56.388 --> 00:25:59.491 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when I say appropriately,
for the average adult,

00:25:59.491 --> 00:26:03.562
it's somewhere between 65
to 67 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:26:03.562 --> 00:26:07.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I know sounds cold,
but cold it must be.

00:26:07.032 --> 00:26:09.901 align:start size:97% position:3%
If we can get there, usually
better sleep unfolds.

00:26:09.901 --> 00:26:12.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. I mean, I'm completely
with you on that one.

00:26:12.404 --> 00:26:14.205
We usually do probably like 68,

00:26:14.205 --> 00:26:16.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is close
enough to that range.

00:26:16.675 --> 00:26:18.610
I mean, it's pretty
chilly in that room,

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:20.145
and that's the number

00:26:20.145 --> 00:26:21.680
'cause there's nothing
worse than waking up

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:24.082
in the middle of the night and
you're hot and sweaty and...

00:26:24.082 --> 00:26:25.317
- Oh yeah, it's a disaster.

00:26:25.317 --> 00:26:27.352
And that changes
your sleep cycles

00:26:27.352 --> 00:26:29.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the quality of your
sleep as well, temperature.

00:26:29.654 --> 00:26:32.090
- All right, let's
talk about marijuana.

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:35.760
Where does marijuana fit
into the equation here?

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:38.730
Because every now and
again, not that often,

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:42.200
I take basically like a puff
or two once or twice a week,

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:44.736
but it's only at night

00:26:44.736 --> 00:26:46.404
when I know I'm going
to fall asleep soon.

00:26:46.404 --> 00:26:48.873
So I got "Seinfeld" on, and
I'm laying on the couch,

00:26:48.873 --> 00:26:50.342
and it's like one puff,

00:26:50.342 --> 00:26:53.011
and then once I feel the
eyes go heavy, I go to bed.

00:26:53.011 --> 00:26:54.279
That's what I try to do.

00:26:54.279 --> 00:26:55.480
Every now and again, I
pass out on the couch,

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:57.182 align:start size:84% position:16%
but what do you
think about marijuana

00:26:57.182 --> 00:26:58.650
when it comes to sleep?

00:26:58.650 --> 00:26:59.718
- I love it.

00:26:59.718 --> 00:27:01.286
"Seinfeld" and a
bit of marijuana

00:27:01.286 --> 00:27:05.123 align:start size:97% position:3%
is sort of the day Rubin
prescription for good sleep.

00:27:05.123 --> 00:27:06.891 align:start size:97% position:3%
- There might've been a
glass of red wine in there,

00:27:06.891 --> 00:27:08.493
which I'll ask you about
that one, too, or you can

00:27:08.493 --> 00:27:09.728
just take it away from there.
- Yeah, we'll come onto that.

00:27:09.728 --> 00:27:11.863 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah, but you'll have
to send me that T-shirt.

00:27:11.863 --> 00:27:16.234
So unfortunately,
it's not great news

00:27:16.234 --> 00:27:18.336
when it comes to marijuana,

00:27:18.336 --> 00:27:21.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I want to
separate THC from CBD.

00:27:21.973 --> 00:27:26.277
So cannabis has a composition
of many different compounds.

00:27:26.277 --> 00:27:28.480
THC or tetrahydrocannabinol

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:32.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
is the psychoactive
component of cannabis.

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:34.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's the thing that
gets people high.

00:27:34.152 --> 00:27:36.221
CBD is different.

00:27:36.221 --> 00:27:39.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is the non-psychoactive
component.

00:27:39.524 --> 00:27:42.494
What we found is
that cannabis use,

00:27:42.494 --> 00:27:46.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
it can speed the time with
which you fall asleep.

00:27:46.331 --> 00:27:47.532
It's speeding up that.

00:27:47.532 --> 00:27:48.967
So in other words,

00:27:48.967 --> 00:27:51.236 align:start size:97% position:3%
it takes you a shorter amount
of time to fall asleep,

00:27:51.236 --> 00:27:54.673
but there are two problems
with cannabis use and sleep.

00:27:54.673 --> 00:27:56.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
Firstly, you develop
a dependency,

00:27:56.741 --> 00:27:59.244
and it develops quite quickly.

00:27:59.244 --> 00:28:00.779
And then when you stop,

00:28:00.779 --> 00:28:04.349
typically what you do is have
terrible rebound insomnia,

00:28:04.349 --> 00:28:06.351
and you have to jump back
on the bandwagon again

00:28:06.351 --> 00:28:08.286
of cannabis use.

00:28:08.286 --> 00:28:11.890
The second issue with THC
is that it's very good

00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:15.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
at disrupting and
suppressing your REM sleep,

00:28:15.026 --> 00:28:16.561
your dream sleep.

00:28:16.561 --> 00:28:19.230
And REM sleep, dream sleep
is associated with things

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:21.366 align:start size:84% position:16%
such as your emotional
and mental health,

00:28:21.366 --> 00:28:25.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
things such as creativity
and memory benefits.

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:29.140 align:start size:81% position:19%
Even your lifespan
is predicted by

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:31.076
the amount of REM
sleep that you get.

00:28:31.076 --> 00:28:33.144
It's also are critical
for hormonal regulation,

00:28:33.144 --> 00:28:35.180
testosterone in
men, for example.

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:38.383
So you really don't want to
have anything that disrupts

00:28:38.383 --> 00:28:41.319
your REM sleep, but
THC will do that.

00:28:41.319 --> 00:28:42.887
So I'm really not...

00:28:42.887 --> 00:28:45.724
You know, again, I feel
when it comes to coffee,

00:28:45.724 --> 00:28:47.859 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm going to make
things even worse for myself

00:28:47.859 --> 00:28:49.027
when it comes to alcohol,

00:28:49.027 --> 00:28:52.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm a desperately
unpopular person

00:28:52.197 --> 00:28:54.566
by both nationality
and personality,

00:28:54.566 --> 00:28:56.668 align:start size:84% position:16%
but when I start
offering these things,

00:28:56.668 --> 00:29:00.705 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm even less sort
of interesting.

00:29:00.705 --> 00:29:03.541 align:start size:81% position:19%
So THC, I would
stay away from it.

00:29:03.541 --> 00:29:06.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
CBD, however, and we don't
have enough data yet,

00:29:06.578 --> 00:29:09.414
nowhere near enough data
to say anything definitive,

00:29:09.414 --> 00:29:10.682
but it looks interesting.

00:29:10.682 --> 00:29:12.951
I think what we can say
about CBD so far is that

00:29:12.951 --> 00:29:16.488
it at least doesn't
harm your sleep.

00:29:16.488 --> 00:29:19.958
There are some signs that
it could help your sleep.

00:29:19.958 --> 00:29:22.260
And I think there are
a number of routes,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:24.929
biological routes,
that are tenable

00:29:24.929 --> 00:29:28.733
through which CBD could
act as a sleep promoter.

00:29:28.733 --> 00:29:31.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
The first is that CBD alters
your body temperature,

00:29:31.803 --> 00:29:36.674
and it will produce a state
of increased hypothermia.

00:29:36.674 --> 00:29:39.477 align:start size:88% position:13%
In other words, it will
help cool you down.

00:29:39.477 --> 00:29:40.411
The second thing is that THC,

00:29:40.411 --> 00:29:42.714
and are some great studies now

00:29:42.714 --> 00:29:44.249
demonstrating that
it's a very strong

00:29:44.249 --> 00:29:46.017
what we call anxiolytic,

00:29:46.017 --> 00:29:48.019
meaning it makes you
feel less anxious.

00:29:48.019 --> 00:29:50.388
If you can cool the body down,

00:29:50.388 --> 00:29:52.557
and you can switch
the body away from

00:29:52.557 --> 00:29:54.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
the fight or flight branch
of the nervous system,

00:29:54.726 --> 00:29:57.662
in other words, the anxious
branch of the nervous system,

00:29:57.662 --> 00:30:00.431 align:start size:94% position:6%
then you are well on
your way to better sleep.

00:30:00.431 --> 00:30:05.303
So I can see why there are at
least two parsimonious reasons

00:30:05.303 --> 00:30:07.672
why CBD could have a benefit.

00:30:07.672 --> 00:30:08.907
Do we have the data yet

00:30:08.907 --> 00:30:10.074
where someone like me would feel

00:30:10.074 --> 00:30:12.277
comfortable advocating that?

00:30:12.277 --> 00:30:13.778
Not right now.

00:30:13.778 --> 00:30:16.281
The only thing I would note
is that the evidence suggests

00:30:16.281 --> 00:30:18.583
CBD is interesting in its curve.

00:30:18.583 --> 00:30:21.920 line:0%
And what I mean by
that is at low doses,

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:25.957 line:0%
CBD may actually
be wake promoting

00:30:25.957 --> 00:30:28.393
and force you awake and
make your sleep worse.

00:30:28.393 --> 00:30:32.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
But at higher doses, it
seems to be more soporific

00:30:32.030 --> 00:30:32.964
in that sense.

00:30:32.964 --> 00:30:34.732
Again, we don't have the data,

00:30:34.732 --> 00:30:36.501
I am not a medical doctor,

00:30:36.501 --> 00:30:38.636
and none of this is
prescriptive advice.

00:30:38.636 --> 00:30:41.039
It's just the science
that we have so far.

00:30:41.039 --> 00:30:42.941
But I would say that
people should just be

00:30:42.941 --> 00:30:44.642
a little bit mindful here.

00:30:44.642 --> 00:30:46.477 align:start size:84% position:16%
Again, the dose
can make the poison

00:30:46.477 --> 00:30:48.313
when it comes to sleep perhaps.

00:30:48.313 --> 00:30:50.014
- All right, now the
wine part of this,

00:30:50.014 --> 00:30:52.116 align:start size:84% position:16%
or we can answer this
with all alcohol,

00:30:52.116 --> 00:30:53.852 align:start size:81% position:19%
but wine, we've
all sat at a table

00:30:53.852 --> 00:30:55.386 align:start size:81% position:19%
with someone who's
drinking wine,

00:30:55.386 --> 00:30:57.255
and at some point, they're
basically falling asleep

00:30:57.255 --> 00:31:00.725
at the table, so how could
that not be good for sleep?

00:31:00.725 --> 00:31:04.295
- Yeah, so alcohol is
not great for sleep

00:31:04.295 --> 00:31:05.830
in three different ways.

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:08.199
Firstly, alcohol is
in a class of drugs

00:31:08.199 --> 00:31:10.335
that we call the sedatives.

00:31:10.335 --> 00:31:12.837
And sedation is not sleep,

00:31:12.837 --> 00:31:15.640
but when you have a couple
of drinks in the evening,

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:18.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you think, "Well, I
fell asleep more quickly,"

00:31:18.743 --> 00:31:22.046
you're mistaking
sedation for sleep,

00:31:22.046 --> 00:31:23.514
and then not the same thing.

00:31:23.514 --> 00:31:25.950
If I were to show you your
electrical brainwave activity

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:27.919
when you've had a couple
of drinks in the evening

00:31:27.919 --> 00:31:29.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
during sleep versus
natural sleep,

00:31:29.854 --> 00:31:31.522
they're not the same thing.

00:31:31.522 --> 00:31:32.857
The second problem with alcohol

00:31:32.857 --> 00:31:34.726
is that it fragments your sleep,

00:31:34.726 --> 00:31:38.162
so you wake up many more
times throughout the night.

00:31:38.162 --> 00:31:39.397
Now the problem here is that

00:31:39.397 --> 00:31:42.133 align:start size:84% position:16%
you will typically not
remember waking up.

00:31:42.133 --> 00:31:44.769
So the next morning maybe
you don't feel restored

00:31:44.769 --> 00:31:46.304
or refreshed by your sleep,

00:31:46.304 --> 00:31:47.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you think, "Well, you
know, I fell asleep quickly,

00:31:47.972 --> 00:31:49.274
and I don't remember waking up,

00:31:49.274 --> 00:31:50.842
so it's not the alcohol,"

00:31:50.842 --> 00:31:54.345
but it definitely will
just litter your sleep

00:31:54.345 --> 00:31:55.980
with these awakenings.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:32:00.518 align:start size:97% position:3%
The final problem with alcohol
is that, similar to THC,

00:32:00.518 --> 00:32:02.887
but through a very
different mechanism,

00:32:02.887 --> 00:32:06.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
alcohol will also
block your dream sleep.

00:32:06.824 --> 00:32:08.426
So, you know, again,

00:32:08.426 --> 00:32:11.462 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm not trying to tell
anyone what they should do,

00:32:11.462 --> 00:32:15.633
and you know, again,
live life to a full

00:32:15.633 --> 00:32:18.036
because I've heard that
we're dead a long time.

00:32:19.003 --> 00:32:21.072
But you know, if I were to give

00:32:21.072 --> 00:32:23.274 align:start size:84% position:16%
politically incorrect
advice, you know,

00:32:23.274 --> 00:32:25.610
if it's just you and me now
talking, Dave, and no one-

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:26.444
- Just us.

00:32:26.444 --> 00:32:27.278
Just us, don't worry.

00:32:27.278 --> 00:32:28.346
- No one's listening.

00:32:28.346 --> 00:32:29.681
The best advice I would have is

00:32:29.681 --> 00:32:31.215
go to the pub in the morning,

00:32:31.215 --> 00:32:33.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that way the alcohol
is out of your system

00:32:33.451 --> 00:32:35.820 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the evening, and
there's no worry at all.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:38.923 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I would never say
that publicly on a show.

00:32:38.923 --> 00:32:40.124
- Got it.

00:32:40.124 --> 00:32:41.059
So you go to the
pub in the morning,

00:32:41.059 --> 00:32:42.560
so you can take that 3:00 PM,

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:44.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
although I guess it's not
quite the nap that you want,

00:32:44.262 --> 00:32:45.830
you're getting more
of the other thing.

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:46.831
- Right, stick it out,

00:32:46.831 --> 00:32:48.333
last it through,

00:32:48.333 --> 00:32:50.034
and then you'll probably be
washed out by the evening.

00:32:50.034 --> 00:32:51.970
- So where does diet
fit into all of this

00:32:51.970 --> 00:32:54.205 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause I think we've all
had different moments

00:32:54.205 --> 00:32:56.674
where, you know, you eat
a real rich, heavy meal

00:32:56.674 --> 00:32:58.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
and suddenly you're not
necessarily sleeping well,

00:32:58.643 --> 00:33:00.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're maybe getting some
indigestion, some heartburn,

00:33:00.011 --> 00:33:01.179
that kind of stuff?

00:33:01.179 --> 00:33:02.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
That obviously
doesn't help sleep,

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:03.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm sure there's
some other foods

00:33:03.848 --> 00:33:07.618 align:start position:0%
that probably aid sleep and
foods that probably harm sleep.

00:33:07.618 --> 00:33:10.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
- There's definitely
a bi-directional
relationship for sure.

00:33:10.822 --> 00:33:13.491 align:start size:88% position:13%
Firstly, what we know
is that eating to excess

00:33:13.491 --> 00:33:18.496
in the evening and also eating
a diet that is high in sugar

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:20.832
and low in fiber,

00:33:20.832 --> 00:33:23.167
both of those things
have been associated

00:33:23.167 --> 00:33:24.869
with significantly
worse sleep quantity

00:33:24.869 --> 00:33:28.272 align:start size:84% position:16%
and significantly
worse sleep quality.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
There is a bit of a myth
though around this, you know,

00:33:31.709 --> 00:33:35.646
you need to stop eating after
four hours or three hours.

00:33:35.646 --> 00:33:36.948
What we found is that you can go

00:33:36.948 --> 00:33:39.951 align:start size:81% position:19%
as close as 60
minutes before bed

00:33:39.951 --> 00:33:42.186
and food doesn't seem
to be a significant

00:33:42.186 --> 00:33:43.821
disruptor of sleep.

00:33:43.821 --> 00:33:46.624
Now, after that, once you
get too close to sleep,

00:33:46.624 --> 00:33:47.992
then it does have a blast radius

00:33:47.992 --> 00:33:51.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
where it can be
detrimental to your sleep.

00:33:51.295 --> 00:33:54.432 align:start size:97% position:3%
There are certain different
sort of food types

00:33:54.432 --> 00:33:56.601
that may enhance sleep,

00:33:56.601 --> 00:33:59.170
and this comes on to sort of
some of the supplement world.

00:33:59.170 --> 00:34:00.872
And I'm a hard nosed scientist,

00:34:00.872 --> 00:34:04.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
and again, I don't
advocate for supplements,

00:34:04.142 --> 00:34:05.610
but it's interesting.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:07.745 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've been doing a lot of
sort of reading and research

00:34:07.745 --> 00:34:09.614
around the supplement space,

00:34:09.614 --> 00:34:11.783
you know, and there's
some fascinating work

00:34:11.783 --> 00:34:14.585 align:start size:81% position:19%
on things like
tart cherry juice.

00:34:14.585 --> 00:34:16.254 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, I was thinking
this is nonsense,

00:34:16.254 --> 00:34:18.923
and I was ready to read the
papers and rip them apart,

00:34:18.923 --> 00:34:20.591
the scientific data,

00:34:20.591 --> 00:34:21.926
but paper after paper

00:34:21.926 --> 00:34:24.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
from some very
well-respected scientists

00:34:24.629 --> 00:34:27.231
seem to show that
it had a benefit.

00:34:27.231 --> 00:34:31.202
Even kiwifruit seemed
to have a benefit.

00:34:31.202 --> 00:34:34.705
Now, why is this the case
for some of these studies?

00:34:34.705 --> 00:34:36.207 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't think it's
because there's anything

00:34:36.207 --> 00:34:38.009
necessarily magical about them.

00:34:38.009 --> 00:34:42.046
It's that they are
high in antioxidants,

00:34:42.046 --> 00:34:45.616
and what we found is that when
you administer antioxidants,

00:34:45.616 --> 00:34:49.053
it has a beneficial
effect on sleep.

00:34:49.053 --> 00:34:51.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so when you're getting
your sufficient supply

00:34:51.789 --> 00:34:53.758
of those key ingredients,

00:34:53.758 --> 00:34:56.427
it seems to communicate
a signal to your brain

00:34:56.427 --> 00:34:58.529
for better sleep at night.

00:34:58.529 --> 00:35:01.833
But overall, I think when
it comes to diet, you know,

00:35:01.833 --> 00:35:03.334
staying away...

00:35:03.334 --> 00:35:05.403
Well, a diet that's high
in sugar and low in fiber

00:35:05.403 --> 00:35:07.939
is pretty much bad for
just about anything,

00:35:07.939 --> 00:35:11.075
but it turns out sleep is
part of that problem too.

00:35:11.075 --> 00:35:12.443
And it works both
ways, by the way.

00:35:12.443 --> 00:35:15.012 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you're not getting
sufficient sleep,

00:35:15.012 --> 00:35:16.914
you will gain more weight,

00:35:16.914 --> 00:35:18.416
and there are a number
of reasons for this.

00:35:18.416 --> 00:35:20.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
Firstly, your appetite
regulating hormones

00:35:20.785 --> 00:35:22.787
go in very bad directions

00:35:22.787 --> 00:35:24.989 align:start size:88% position:13%
when you're not getting
sufficient sleep.

00:35:24.989 --> 00:35:27.859 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you won't feel satisfied
by what you're eating,

00:35:27.859 --> 00:35:29.427
and you'll want to eat more

00:35:29.427 --> 00:35:31.095 align:start size:81% position:19%
because you're
constantly hungry.

00:35:31.095 --> 00:35:34.232 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's basically the sleep
deprivation induced munchies.

00:35:34.232 --> 00:35:35.900
And it's not just
those hormones.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:38.336
Coming back to cannabis,

00:35:38.336 --> 00:35:42.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
your body produces its own
version of those cannabinoids.

00:35:42.006 --> 00:35:44.408
They're called endocannabinoids.

00:35:44.408 --> 00:35:46.911
And most people have
sort of had or heard,

00:35:46.911 --> 00:35:48.412
I'm sure you have
not experienced,

00:35:48.412 --> 00:35:51.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
but when you have a little
too much just to smoke,

00:35:51.282 --> 00:35:52.884 align:start size:84% position:16%
you get the munchies.
- Once.

00:35:52.884 --> 00:35:55.086
- Yeah, and you start just
wanting to consume everything

00:35:55.086 --> 00:35:58.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
because these cannabinoids
increase your appetite.

00:35:58.723 --> 00:36:00.725 align:start size:81% position:19%
They increase your
desire to eat.

00:36:00.725 --> 00:36:02.860
Well, when you are under slept,

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:04.362
when you're sleep deprived,

00:36:04.362 --> 00:36:08.466 align:start position:0%
your body increases its own
production of endocannabinoids.

00:36:08.466 --> 00:36:10.801 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's part of the reason
why you start to feel

00:36:10.801 --> 00:36:13.271
so much more hungry when
you are sleep deprived

00:36:13.271 --> 00:36:15.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
and why you will eat
somewhere between

00:36:15.673 --> 00:36:18.309
two to 400 extra
calories each day

00:36:18.309 --> 00:36:19.977 align:start size:88% position:13%
when you're not getting
sufficient sleep.

00:36:19.977 --> 00:36:21.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Wow, that's
really interesting.

00:36:21.312 --> 00:36:23.114
Let's wrap up with dreams

00:36:23.114 --> 00:36:26.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
because this will be my
fifth August off the grid,

00:36:26.651 --> 00:36:29.487
and one of the things
that I've noticed is,

00:36:29.487 --> 00:36:32.523
because I try to work
on exercise and diet

00:36:32.523 --> 00:36:35.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
and sleeping better and
just general digital detox

00:36:35.326 --> 00:36:37.061
and relaxation,

00:36:37.061 --> 00:36:39.230
I've noticed in the
four previous years
that I've done this

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:42.166
that my dreams are more
vivid during that time.

00:36:42.166 --> 00:36:46.337
Maybe it's from being less
distracted or something.

00:36:46.337 --> 00:36:47.572
So just broadly,

00:36:47.572 --> 00:36:49.907
what the hell is going
on when we're dreaming,

00:36:49.907 --> 00:36:53.244
and what can we
do to, I suppose,

00:36:53.244 --> 00:36:56.113 align:start size:88% position:13%
maybe gain more
insight from our dreams

00:36:56.113 --> 00:36:58.349
or something to that effect?

00:36:58.349 --> 00:37:00.284
- Yeah, and actually I have
a quick question for you.

00:37:00.284 --> 00:37:02.286
So when you're doing the detox,

00:37:02.286 --> 00:37:04.121
will your sleep schedule change

00:37:04.121 --> 00:37:07.325
where you may start to go
to bed a little bit later

00:37:07.325 --> 00:37:08.559
and wake up a little bit later?

00:37:08.559 --> 00:37:10.494 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No. I'll be going
to bed earlier usually

00:37:10.494 --> 00:37:13.331
because I'm not doing
as much during the day.

00:37:13.331 --> 00:37:15.233
So, you know, for
a portion of this,

00:37:15.233 --> 00:37:17.768
and perhaps right now
as this is being aired,

00:37:17.768 --> 00:37:19.437
I'm hoping that
I'm on the beach,

00:37:19.437 --> 00:37:20.938 align:start size:81% position:19%
and it's a nice
bright, sunny day,

00:37:20.938 --> 00:37:23.474
and I'm under a umbrella,
and it's about 3:00 PM,

00:37:23.474 --> 00:37:24.275
and I'm napping.

00:37:24.275 --> 00:37:25.776
That would be nice.

00:37:25.776 --> 00:37:27.245
But then, you know, after
a day out on the beach,

00:37:27.245 --> 00:37:30.314 align:start position:0%
by nine o'clock, there's a good
chance I could be sleeping.

00:37:30.314 --> 00:37:31.549
And then I'm still,

00:37:31.549 --> 00:37:34.452
regardless of when I
go to sleep at night,

00:37:34.452 --> 00:37:35.686
I'm an early riser.

00:37:35.686 --> 00:37:37.388
That definitely is
something new to me

00:37:37.388 --> 00:37:39.490
the last couple of years of
my life, where it's like...

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
And maybe that's
partly sunshine in LA.

00:37:41.492 --> 00:37:44.895 align:start position:0%
You know, it's sunny here
usually at about six or 6:30 AM,

00:37:44.895 --> 00:37:47.598
and I'm usually just kind of
ready to go at that point,

00:37:47.598 --> 00:37:51.302 align:start position:0%
but I probably will get a
significant amount more of sleep

00:37:51.302 --> 00:37:53.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the course of
these 30 days or so.

00:37:53.537 --> 00:37:55.072
- Got it.

00:37:55.072 --> 00:37:58.342
So firstly, to stick a pin
in the daylight component,

00:37:58.342 --> 00:38:02.446
daylight will absolutely
regulate your sleep timing,

00:38:02.446 --> 00:38:04.081
and you can use it on both ends.

00:38:04.081 --> 00:38:06.450
If you get too much light
exposure in the evening,

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:10.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
then it will block a
hormone called melatonin,

00:38:10.087 --> 00:38:12.857
which is critical to
signal to your brain

00:38:12.857 --> 00:38:15.159
it's nighttime, and
it's time to sleep.

00:38:15.159 --> 00:38:17.161
And so when we have too
much electric lights,

00:38:17.161 --> 00:38:18.996
or we're using our
computers at night,

00:38:18.996 --> 00:38:21.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
it stamps the
brakes on melatonin,

00:38:21.098 --> 00:38:22.767
and that's why some
people can struggle

00:38:22.767 --> 00:38:24.268
with their at night.

00:38:24.268 --> 00:38:26.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
So try to dim down half
of the lights in your home

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:28.472
in the last hour before sleep.

00:38:28.472 --> 00:38:31.075 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then you can
reverse engineer the
trick in the morning.

00:38:31.075 --> 00:38:32.243
Get lots of daylight.

00:38:32.243 --> 00:38:34.645 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, LA is a
great place to do that.

00:38:34.645 --> 00:38:37.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it enlivens
you because it now,

00:38:37.381 --> 00:38:40.051
sort of that daylight
will apply once again

00:38:40.051 --> 00:38:43.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
the brakes on melatonin, and
it will help you wake up.

00:38:43.521 --> 00:38:46.924
But coming back to dreams
and the function of dreams,

00:38:46.924 --> 00:38:49.327
dreams seem to, or REM
sleep dreaming at least,

00:38:49.327 --> 00:38:51.762
seems to provide at least
two different benefits.

00:38:51.762 --> 00:38:54.265
The first is creativity.

00:38:54.265 --> 00:38:57.068
What we found is that
it's during dream sleep

00:38:57.068 --> 00:38:59.337
when the brain takes
all of the information

00:38:59.337 --> 00:39:00.771
that it's been learning,

00:39:00.771 --> 00:39:02.540
and it starts to
fuse it together

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:04.775
in creative and innovative ways.

00:39:04.775 --> 00:39:08.145
You know, it's almost like
an informational alchemy.

00:39:08.145 --> 00:39:11.582
And so we go to sleep with
the pieces of the jigsaw,

00:39:11.582 --> 00:39:14.585
but we wake up with
the puzzle complete,

00:39:14.585 --> 00:39:17.455 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's one of the
creative benefits of sleep.

00:39:17.455 --> 00:39:20.458
And there's a reason that
no one has ever told you

00:39:20.458 --> 00:39:21.692
to stay awake on a problem.

00:39:21.692 --> 00:39:23.294
(Dave laughing)

00:39:23.294 --> 00:39:27.598
And there's a very
good scientific basis
for exactly that.

00:39:27.598 --> 00:39:30.000 align:start size:97% position:3%
The second interesting
thing that we've discovered,

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:32.103
and we've done quite a
lot of work in this area,

00:39:32.103 --> 00:39:34.305
is that REM sleep
dreaming provides

00:39:34.305 --> 00:39:37.808
a form of emotional first aid,

00:39:37.808 --> 00:39:42.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you can think of dreaming
as overnight therapy.

00:39:42.079 --> 00:39:45.316
And REM sleep dreams will take
those difficult experiences,

00:39:45.316 --> 00:39:47.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
sometimes even
traumatic experiences

00:39:47.852 --> 00:39:49.019
that you've been having,

00:39:49.019 --> 00:39:51.322 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it acts like a
nocturnal soothing balm,

00:39:51.322 --> 00:39:53.724
and it just takes
the sharp edges off

00:39:53.724 --> 00:39:55.793 align:start size:84% position:16%
those painful,
difficult experiences

00:39:55.793 --> 00:39:57.528
so that you come
back the next day,

00:39:57.528 --> 00:40:00.431
and they don't feel
as painful anymore.

00:40:00.431 --> 00:40:03.134 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I think in
that sense, dreaming,

00:40:03.134 --> 00:40:04.835
you can think of it as,

00:40:04.835 --> 00:40:08.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
well, it's not time
that heals all wounds,

00:40:08.038 --> 00:40:10.441 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's time during dream
sleep that provides

00:40:10.441 --> 00:40:13.344
emotional convalescence.

00:40:13.344 --> 00:40:14.945
And those are the two functions

00:40:14.945 --> 00:40:16.914 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we've been
strongly associating

00:40:16.914 --> 00:40:18.382
with REM sleep dreaming.

00:40:18.382 --> 00:40:20.384 align:start size:97% position:3%
- What about people that
don't remember their dreams?

00:40:20.384 --> 00:40:21.685
I always find it so odd

00:40:21.685 --> 00:40:23.387
because I really do
remember my dreams.

00:40:23.387 --> 00:40:24.622
Every now and again,

00:40:24.622 --> 00:40:26.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'll have a notepad
by the side of my bed,

00:40:26.257 --> 00:40:28.092
and I'll scribble something
down that I want to remember

00:40:28.092 --> 00:40:29.326
for the next morning,
something like that.

00:40:29.326 --> 00:40:31.462
And I do find that
there are insights,

00:40:31.462 --> 00:40:33.731 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I can piece
together something

00:40:33.731 --> 00:40:36.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
that maybe I couldn't
have otherwise.

00:40:36.167 --> 00:40:38.135 align:start position:0%
What about people that just
have no memory of their dreams?

00:40:38.135 --> 00:40:40.070
You hear about that
every now and again?

00:40:40.070 --> 00:40:41.272
- Yeah, it's not infrequent.

00:40:41.272 --> 00:40:43.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's not necessarily
pathological.

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:45.176
It's not something that
you have to worry about

00:40:45.176 --> 00:40:47.611 line:0%
or go and see your doctor about.

00:40:47.611 --> 00:40:50.781 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
We don't truly
understand why there are

00:40:50.781 --> 00:40:52.750 line:0%
those individual differences.

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:54.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
We certainly know
that they are present,

00:40:54.318 --> 00:40:55.953
but we also know that
those people who say,

00:40:55.953 --> 00:40:57.855
"Look, I just don't dream,"

00:40:57.855 --> 00:40:59.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's not really that
you don't dream,

00:40:59.423 --> 00:41:01.625
as you said, it's that you
don't remember your dreams

00:41:01.625 --> 00:41:04.128
because if I bring those
people into my sleep center

00:41:04.128 --> 00:41:06.230
here at University of
California, Berkeley,

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:09.300 align:start position:0%
and we can clearly see that
they're going into dream sleep.

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:11.635
And in fact, if I
go into the bedroom

00:41:11.635 --> 00:41:13.904
when they're in a
REM sleep phase,

00:41:13.904 --> 00:41:15.406
and I wake them up, and I say,

00:41:15.406 --> 00:41:17.174
"Can you just tell me what
was going through your mind

00:41:17.174 --> 00:41:18.943
before I woke you up?'

00:41:18.943 --> 00:41:20.411
They will say, "It's
the strangest thing.

00:41:20.411 --> 00:41:22.046
I was having a dream
for the first time."

00:41:22.046 --> 00:41:24.715
It's just that when they
wake up in the morning,

00:41:24.715 --> 00:41:27.418
that memory seems to
evaporate too quickly,

00:41:27.418 --> 00:41:29.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it doesn't seem
to be committed

00:41:29.487 --> 00:41:31.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
to conscious memory
recollection.

00:41:31.889 --> 00:41:35.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
Does it mean that that
memory isn't there at all?

00:41:35.459 --> 00:41:37.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually don't think
that that's true.

00:41:37.595 --> 00:41:39.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's quite
possible that we remember

00:41:39.864 --> 00:41:42.032
much more of our dreaming lives

00:41:42.032 --> 00:41:44.001
than we are consciously aware of

00:41:44.001 --> 00:41:47.071 align:start size:94% position:6%
or that we have conscious
accessibility to.

00:41:47.071 --> 00:41:50.407 align:start position:0%
But what we know from brain
science is that implicit memory,

00:41:50.407 --> 00:41:53.210
information that you're
not consciously aware of,

00:41:53.210 --> 00:41:55.513
can still profoundly
change your decisions

00:41:55.513 --> 00:41:57.748
and your actions
and your behaviors.

00:41:57.748 --> 00:41:59.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it leads to a
wacky hypothesis that

00:41:59.483 --> 00:42:03.053
in fact, you know, much
more of our waking life

00:42:03.053 --> 00:42:07.191
is determined by our
imprinted dream life,

00:42:07.191 --> 00:42:10.127 align:start position:0%
but we just don't have conscious
access to that dreaming.

00:42:10.127 --> 00:42:11.395 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I say that
because, you know,

00:42:11.395 --> 00:42:12.897
I'm sure you've had
that experience too,

00:42:12.897 --> 00:42:14.999
where you wake up and you
think, "I was dreaming.

00:42:16.333 --> 00:42:17.167 align:start size:97% position:3%
What was that dream?"
- [Dave] What was that? Yeah.

00:42:17.167 --> 00:42:18.402
- And it's just gone.

00:42:18.402 --> 00:42:19.970
And then two days
later, you know,

00:42:19.970 --> 00:42:22.339
you getting into your car,
and you see the key chain,

00:42:22.339 --> 00:42:24.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it instantly
triggers the unlocking

00:42:24.808 --> 00:42:27.244
of the memory of that dream.

00:42:27.244 --> 00:42:29.847 align:start size:88% position:13%
In other words, that
memory was always there.

00:42:29.847 --> 00:42:33.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
You'd lost the IP
address to go and find it

00:42:33.551 --> 00:42:35.719
for conscious recollection.

00:42:35.719 --> 00:42:37.021
And if it's still there,

00:42:37.021 --> 00:42:39.323 align:start size:88% position:13%
it could still be
changing your behavior.

00:42:39.323 --> 00:42:41.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
- All right, I said the
dreams was the last question,

00:42:41.525 --> 00:42:44.161 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'll give myself one
bonus question here, which is,

00:42:44.161 --> 00:42:46.263 align:start size:81% position:19%
do you have one
other tip or trick

00:42:46.263 --> 00:42:48.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I can incorporate
into this month

00:42:48.599 --> 00:42:51.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that I come back as
well-rested and ready to go

00:42:51.969 --> 00:42:55.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
and be back in the crazy
political and cultural fight

00:42:55.406 --> 00:42:58.676
that I find myself in 11
months out of the year?

00:42:58.676 --> 00:43:01.412
- You are a brave, brave
man, knowing what you do,

00:43:01.412 --> 00:43:02.913
and I admire it greatly.

00:43:02.913 --> 00:43:05.783
I am far too thin skinned
to be doing what you do.

00:43:05.783 --> 00:43:07.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You are definitely
a wiser man, so.

00:43:07.818 --> 00:43:09.853
- Let's not rush to judgment.

00:43:09.853 --> 00:43:11.088
I probably, in the
past 45 minutes,

00:43:11.088 --> 00:43:12.957
have just proven that
to be wrong as well,

00:43:12.957 --> 00:43:15.359
but I will give you
one conventional tip

00:43:15.359 --> 00:43:16.794
and one unconventional tip.

00:43:16.794 --> 00:43:17.962
- Great.

00:43:17.962 --> 00:43:19.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
- The first conventional
tip is regularity.

00:43:19.430 --> 00:43:22.833 align:start size:97% position:3%
If I could give you one
single thing to start doing,

00:43:22.833 --> 00:43:25.736
go to bed at the same time,
wake up at the same time,

00:43:25.736 --> 00:43:28.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
no matter whether it's the
weekday or the weekend,

00:43:28.539 --> 00:43:30.674
and regularity is king.

00:43:30.674 --> 00:43:33.777
And the reason, because I
don't think people respond

00:43:33.777 --> 00:43:35.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
to rules that are
just barked at them,

00:43:35.879 --> 00:43:38.148 align:start size:88% position:13%
they respond to the
reasons, not the rules.

00:43:38.148 --> 00:43:39.383
And the reason is this,

00:43:39.383 --> 00:43:42.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
that your brain has
its own 24 hour clock,

00:43:42.953 --> 00:43:46.523
and it responds best to sort of

00:43:46.523 --> 00:43:48.926
the recognition of regularity.

00:43:48.926 --> 00:43:50.928
So when it's getting regularity,

00:43:50.928 --> 00:43:52.730
it's going to anchor your sleep,

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:54.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
and as I said,
improve the quantity

00:43:54.298 --> 00:43:56.033
and the quality of that sleep.

00:43:56.033 --> 00:43:59.269
So aim for regularity no
matter what day it is.

00:43:59.269 --> 00:44:01.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
In terms of an
unconventional tip,

00:44:01.605 --> 00:44:03.307
and this may sound a
little bit strange,

00:44:03.307 --> 00:44:07.277
but if you have a bad night of
sleep, and everyone has them,

00:44:07.277 --> 00:44:08.579
you know, I am not immune

00:44:08.579 --> 00:44:10.280
just because I know a
little bit about sleep.

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:12.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have got my big
share of bad nights,

00:44:12.549 --> 00:44:14.885
and I've had my own
bouts of insomnia

00:44:14.885 --> 00:44:17.254
that I've battled
and struggled with.

00:44:17.254 --> 00:44:20.391
But after a bad night of
sleep, people will say,

00:44:20.391 --> 00:44:21.592
"What should I do?"

00:44:21.592 --> 00:44:24.662
And my answer is do nothing.

00:44:24.662 --> 00:44:28.699
And what I mean by that is
don't wake up any later,

00:44:28.699 --> 00:44:31.602
don't drink any more
coffee the next day,

00:44:31.602 --> 00:44:33.237
don't nap during that day,

00:44:33.237 --> 00:44:38.008 align:start position:0%
and don't go to bed any earlier
than you would otherwise.

00:44:38.008 --> 00:44:39.510
Do nothing.

00:44:39.510 --> 00:44:42.046
Just stay the course and
repeat the same behavior.

00:44:42.046 --> 00:44:43.914
Because if you wake up later,

00:44:43.914 --> 00:44:46.050
then you're not
going to build up

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:49.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
enough of that sleep
pressure that we spoke about

00:44:49.019 --> 00:44:51.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
for you to fall asleep
at your normal time

00:44:51.121 --> 00:44:52.956
because you slept in later.

00:44:52.956 --> 00:44:55.225
And second, if you
go to bed too early,

00:44:55.225 --> 00:44:56.894 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you think
last night was so bad,

00:44:56.894 --> 00:44:58.696
I should get to bed early,

00:44:58.696 --> 00:45:01.932
your body is not expecting to
sleep at that different time,

00:45:01.932 --> 00:45:03.467
that new earlier time,

00:45:03.467 --> 00:45:05.302
so you're just going
to lie in bed awake,

00:45:05.302 --> 00:45:07.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
tossing and turning, and
it's going to be even worse.

00:45:07.838 --> 00:45:10.274
And then obviously,
caffeine and naps

00:45:10.274 --> 00:45:11.709
we've spoken about too.

00:45:11.709 --> 00:45:14.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
So the best
unconventional advice

00:45:14.178 --> 00:45:17.581
if you've had a bad night
of sleep, is do nothing.

00:45:17.581 --> 00:45:19.516
Stick to your routine.

00:45:19.516 --> 00:45:21.351
- Matt, this was a
little unconventional

00:45:21.351 --> 00:45:23.754
for my type of show, but
I thoroughly enjoyed it.

00:45:23.754 --> 00:45:25.622 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is exactly
what I wanted to do

00:45:25.622 --> 00:45:26.890 align:start size:81% position:19%
right before I
went off the grid,

00:45:26.890 --> 00:45:28.792
and I hope that
everyone watching

00:45:28.792 --> 00:45:30.961
is going to do a little
bit of this in August,

00:45:30.961 --> 00:45:32.429
if no other month.

00:45:32.429 --> 00:45:33.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I always do my
off the grid thing in August

00:45:33.864 --> 00:45:35.599 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I feel like
it's end of the summer,

00:45:35.599 --> 00:45:37.000
if there's ever
a time for anyone

00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:38.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
to get their life
in order, that's it,

00:45:38.502 --> 00:45:40.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then hopefully
you can incorporate it

00:45:40.537 --> 00:45:42.272
into those other 11 months.

00:45:42.272 --> 00:45:43.474
I really enjoyed talking to you,

00:45:43.474 --> 00:45:45.242
and I appreciate
you doing the show.

00:45:45.242 --> 00:45:46.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Thank you so
much for having me.

00:45:46.510 --> 00:45:48.011
Take care and good
luck with August.

00:45:48.011 --> 00:45:48.946
- Thanks.

